Dear CFPB Representative, I am filing a formal dispute regarding a fraudulent and inaccurate charge-off account reported by Affirm Inc. on my credit report. This account does not belong to me, and its presence on my credit file constitutes an unlawful violation of my rights under federal law. I am demanding its immediate removal based on the following legal grounds.

Legal Violations and Supporting Case Law Failure to Properly Verify Identity ( FCRA Violation 15 U.S.C. 1681e ( b ) ) Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ), credit reporting agencies ( CRAs ) must ensure that all reported information is accurate and belongs to the correct consumer.

Affirm Inc. failed to verify my identity before associating this account with my credit report. 

Case Law : In XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX ( XXXX XXXX. XXXX ), the court ruled that credit furnishers must ensure accuracy, and failure to do so constitutes a violation of the FCRA. 

Account Belongs to Another Individual ( FDCPA Violation 15 U.S.C. 1692e ( 2 ) ( A ) ) The Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ) prohibits the reporting of false or misleading information regarding a consumers credit.

Reporting a debt that does not belong to me is a direct violation of 15 U.S.C. 1692e ( 2 ) ( A ), which prohibits false representations of a consumer 's debt obligations. 

Case Law : In XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX ( XXXX ), the Supreme Court reinforced that debt collectors and furnishers must prove ownership and validity of a debt before reporting it.

Lack of a Valid Agreement and Payment Records ( Contract Law and FCRA Violation 15 U.S.C. 1681i ) I never signed any agreement with Affirm Inc. and never made any payments toward this alleged debt.

